<!--
    PATCH FILE — AndroidManifest.patch.xml

    Apply this change to: MundoCore/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml

    WHY: GmsHookInstaller.ensureInitialized() is called lazily from bindService hooks.
    However, as a belt-and-suspenders guarantee that the GMS interceptor is set up
    BEFORE the virtual app even calls bindService, register GmsChildProcessProvider
    as a ContentProvider with android:multiprocess="true".

    Android instantiates ContentProviders at process startup. With multiprocess="true",
    a fresh instance is created in each process that accesses it.

    BlackBox queries host-package ContentProviders during virtual process startup, so
    this ContentProvider's onCreate() runs early in the virtual process — before any
    bindService call from the cloned app.

    OPTIONAL (the lazy-init in GmsHookInstaller alone is sufficient).
    Add this inside the <application> tag:
-->

<application>
    <!-- ... existing providers ... -->

    <!--
        GmsChildProcessProvider: early GMS hook init in BlackBox virtual processes.
        Authority must be unique — replace com.yourapp with the host app's package name.
        In a library AAR use a placeholder: ${applicationId}.mundogms
    -->
    <provider
        android:name="com.mundo.proxy.gms.GmsChildProcessProvider"
        android:authorities="${applicationId}.mundogms"
        android:exported="false"
        android:multiprocess="true"
        android:initOrder="100" />

</application>

<!--
    NOTE on android:process:
    Do NOT add android:process here — leaving it unset means Android can instantiate
    this provider in ANY process of the host package, which is exactly what we want.
    With android:multiprocess="true" and no process name, Android creates a fresh
    instance of this ContentProvider in each process that accesses it, including
    BlackBox's virtual processes (com.bgmi:p0, com.bgmi:p1, etc.).

    BlackBox's stub providers already trigger content provider resolution during
    virtual app process startup, so this provider's onCreate() fires early.
-->
